the nodes  begin working on a new block  and basically they show their acceptance  of exactly what block they accept  because they're including the hash of  that previous block in the new block  so if you recall that big time stamp  server from like i think three videos  ago  the timestamps are hashed and each new  block contains the previous blocks hash  timestamp  which basically is including every other  previous blocks timestamp because  if you recall the order if you you know  hashed  the same input in the same order you  would  end up with the same exact hash so  that's that's basically how that  uh how that works and why that works so  you still only have one hash  but it in theory contains every other  hash  within it uh because it it it's based on  that order  of input all right so easy enough just  assume  a few simple rules to the network and  how it works and really that is  you know there are only six rules so  anyway  um back to the paper nodes always  consider  the longest chain to be the correct one  and we'll continue working on extending  that chain if two nodes broadcast  different versions  of the next block simultaneously some  nodes may receive  one and uh or the other first  in that case they work on the first one  that they received but  save the other branch in case it becomes  longer  the tie will be broken when the next  proof of work is found and one branch  becomes  longer the nodes that were working on  the other branch  will then just simply switch to the  longer one  now here is the first example of another  reason why best effort is the chosen  system  um as a rule nodes choose to accept the  longest chain because this is the chain  that clearly is the most supported by  the most  cpu power in the network since to be the  longest it simply means that  it has the most you know quote unquote  cpu votes so to speak  so in the case of two nodes yelling out  a new block at the exact same time  some nodes will get one version other  nodes would get another and they will  both work on achieving  the next block not whoever reaches the  next block  first would be the victor and the nodes  working on the other one would simply  shift over because  again at the broadcasting of a new block  the nodes would realize they were  working on a shorter  invalid chain and they would just hop  over to that new one  so back to the paper again new  transaction broadcasts do not  necessarily need to reach  all nodes as long as they reach many  nodes  they will get into the block before long  block broadcasts  are on are also tolerant of dropped  messages so if a node does not receive a  block it will request it when it  receives  the next block and realizes hey i missed  one  now again best effort is further  utilized here  this also kind of builds on the last  paragraph where simultaneously two  blocks were broadcast and the winner is  the one where the proof of work is  solved first  so if a block is just missed right if  they just somehow didn't get that  message  then that node's going to keep working  on the previous block and then once  another  block is received that node that is  behind is going to realize oops  and it's going to catch up because it's  working on the wrong chain now  so now i want to go ahead and further  break down why  best effort is used here and um  and now again best effort means in case  you skip the abstract  it's an attempt the best effort  basically means there's an attempt to do  something  but if that something doesn't occur or  it's done wrong or whatever there's no  re-attempt  there's no air reporting any of that and  everything just keeps kind of trucking  along  so the main benefit to a best effort  system  is a lot like the main benefit to the  bitcoin network itself  and also a lot of uh or it's a lot like  a type of machine learning called  an artificial neural network so  an artificial neural network is neat  because it contains a principle  known as graceful degradation so  artificial neural network  obviously is also a network uh and the  intent  and the attempt of this is to mimic the  human brain so say you have a task where  you're giving this artificial neural  network and you show it like a picture  of a small  couch let's say now some of the nodes in  the network  uh might call it like a futon or  something like that a few might call it  a bed  and many are gonna call it a chair but  most hopefully  say this is a couch so if the  artificial neural network is working  correctly couch  will have the most votes though still  many of the nodes will be incorrect  possibly even corrupt so this is why  graceful degradation is  nice and why neural networks within  logic of programs is really helpful or  just parallel programming  because normally when you have what's  called linear programming  where it's strictly like step by step  it's literally  linear it's just a line if any of the  steps along the way get messed up  somehow basically everything  fails right you either succeed or you  fail and any degree of failure results  in like  catastrophic failure but with graceful  degradation  and like networking and also what's  called parallel  programming what's used here is if  you're using like a neural network  you might have multiple tasks happening  at the same time multiple calculations  and all this  some can fail some can be wrong some can  have bugs  but as long as the majority are correct  you'll find yourself having success  now a good analogy here is like a rope  versus a chain  so in a chain if if one of the links are  broken the whole chain is  is rendered useless catastrophic failure  but with best effort or neural networks  or  parallel programming you've got  something more like a rope  where the fibers of the rope  individually can break yet the rope  itself is still successful so back to  bitcoin  with voting processing working on new  blocks it's all parallel programming  on a best effort basis so even when some  nodes  either get corrupt like either  mistakenly or purposefully  send wrong data get wrong data get no  data it really doesn't matter  the network itself is based on graceful  degradation  and it's still going to operate  correctly and securely  and this is why best effort is used and  it's there's really a whole  slew of reasons why uh it's used  so that's going to conclude the network  section of the paper  the next section we're going to cover is  incentives so stay tuned for that  uh if you guys are confused or you've  got any questions or comments or  whatever feel free to leave those below  as always thanks for watching thanks for  all the support and the subscriptions  and until next time

